1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
Moving on to valvular disorders,

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,600
let's remind ourselves of some basic anatomy.

3
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:06,300
Of course, the heart has four valves.

4
00:00:06,300 --> 00:00:08,700
Here's the right atrium, right ventricle,

5
00:00:08,700 --> 00:00:11,040
left atrium, and left ventricle.

6
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:13,540
There is a tricuspid valve here.

7
00:00:13,540 --> 00:00:15,980
There is a pulmonary valve here,

8
00:00:15,980 --> 00:00:17,780
a mitral valve here,

9
00:00:17,780 --> 00:00:20,080
and then an aortic valve here.

10
00:00:20,080 --> 00:00:22,220
Now, of course, the idea is that the valves

11
00:00:22,220 --> 00:00:24,460
help to prevent backflow

12
00:00:24,460 --> 00:00:27,220
as blood is being pushed forward through the system,

13
00:00:27,220 --> 00:00:29,300
and the disorders of the valves are basically two.

14
00:00:29,300 --> 00:00:31,700
One is when you get stenosis,

15
00:00:31,700 --> 00:00:35,200
and so, for example, you can get stenosis of the aortic valve.

16
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:38,340
When you get stenosis, that tightens the orifice of the valve,

17
00:00:38,340 --> 00:00:40,520
it narrows the orifice of the valve,

18
00:00:40,520 --> 00:00:42,740
which then makes it harder to push blood through.

19
00:00:42,740 --> 00:00:43,740
The other possibility would be

20
00:00:43,740 --> 00:00:45,240
that you can get regurg of the valve.

21
00:00:45,240 --> 00:00:47,180
For example, if you had mitral valve regurg,

22
00:00:47,180 --> 00:00:49,680
that would allow backflow across the valve,

23
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:51,380
and so these are the two types of pathologies

24
00:00:51,380 --> 00:00:52,960
that are going to arise

25
00:00:52,960 --> 00:00:54,900
as we move forward in our discussion.

26
00:00:54,900 --> 00:00:56,700
The very first topic that we want to cover

27
00:00:56,700 --> 00:00:58,460
is acute rheumatic fever.

28
00:00:58,460 --> 00:01:00,720
Now, this is a systemic complication

29
00:01:00,720 --> 00:01:05,100
of a group A beta hemolytic strep infection of the throat,

30
00:01:05,100 --> 00:01:07,040
usually seen in children two to three weeks

31
00:01:07,040 --> 00:01:08,600
after the strep throat,

32
00:01:08,600 --> 00:01:10,980
and what happens is you generate an immune response

33
00:01:10,980 --> 00:01:12,400
against the bacteria,

34
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:14,340
and the bacteria has M proteins,

35
00:01:14,340 --> 00:01:16,620
and these M proteins are antigens

36
00:01:16,620 --> 00:01:19,720
that resemble antigens in human tissues,

37
00:01:19,720 --> 00:01:21,580
and so, therefore, as you're generating a response

38
00:01:21,580 --> 00:01:22,780
against the M proteins,

39
00:01:22,780 --> 00:01:25,380
you begin to generate a response against human tissues,

40
00:01:25,380 --> 00:01:27,280
and that results in damage to tissues,

41
00:01:27,280 --> 00:01:29,720
and then, of course, acute rheumatic fever.

42
00:01:29,720 --> 00:01:30,660
Now, it's important to note,

43
00:01:30,660 --> 00:01:32,360
in very high yield for exams,

44
00:01:32,360 --> 00:01:35,060
that this notion of the bacterial M protein

45
00:01:35,060 --> 00:01:39,320
resembling human tissues is called molecular mimicry,

46
00:01:39,320 --> 00:01:41,500
and the board examiners like you to know that term,

47
00:01:41,500 --> 00:01:43,560
molecular mimicry.

48
00:01:43,560 --> 00:01:46,200
When you'd like to make a diagnosis of acute rheumatic fever,

49
00:01:46,200 --> 00:01:48,440
you do so using the Jones criteria.

50
00:01:48,440 --> 00:01:50,080
Now, the first thing that you need is evidence

51
00:01:50,080 --> 00:01:52,940
of a prior group A beta hemolytic strep infection.

52
00:01:52,940 --> 00:01:55,780
For example, anti-streptolysin O titers,

53
00:01:55,780 --> 00:01:57,740
or anti-DNA speed titers.

54
00:01:57,740 --> 00:02:00,060
These are both markers of a prior group A

55
00:02:00,060 --> 00:02:02,120
beta hemolytic strep infection.

56
00:02:02,120 --> 00:02:04,260
In addition, you could identify minor criteria,

57
00:02:04,260 --> 00:02:06,960
such as fever and elevated ESR,

58
00:02:06,960 --> 00:02:09,460
and then there's a series of major criteria,

59
00:02:09,460 --> 00:02:11,320
and it's important to know each of them.

60
00:02:11,320 --> 00:02:15,000
Now, I use the first letters of the word Jones

61
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:16,800
in order to remind me of the criteria.

62
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:19,540
Of course, these are called the Jones criteria.

63
00:02:19,540 --> 00:02:22,280
So the J reminds me that you're gonna get joint problems,

64
00:02:22,280 --> 00:02:25,980
and the key problem here would be a migratory polyarthritis,

65
00:02:25,980 --> 00:02:27,640
where you get arthritis of a large joint,

66
00:02:27,640 --> 00:02:30,040
such as the wrist, knees, or ankles,

67
00:02:30,040 --> 00:02:32,680
and then it resolves and moves to another large joint.

68
00:02:32,680 --> 00:02:35,020
The O sort of reminds us that there's going to be

69
00:02:35,020 --> 00:02:38,680
heart problems, and the heart problems are pancarditis,

70
00:02:38,680 --> 00:02:41,300
where you get inflammation of each of the layers of the heart.

71
00:02:41,300 --> 00:02:44,120
And so you get a endocarditis, a myocarditis,

72
00:02:44,120 --> 00:02:45,600
and a pericarditis.

73
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:47,300
And we'll talk about the inflammation of the heart

74
00:02:47,300 --> 00:02:49,340
in a little more detail in a moment.

75
00:02:49,340 --> 00:02:51,640
The N reminds us of the fact that we're going to have

76
00:02:51,640 --> 00:02:56,580
nodules in the skin, so you get subcutaneous nodules.

77
00:02:56,580 --> 00:02:59,720
The E stands for erythema marginatum.

78
00:02:59,720 --> 00:03:03,360
Erythema marginatum is a rash that appears more red

79
00:03:03,360 --> 00:03:04,860
at the margins of the rash.

80
00:03:04,860 --> 00:03:07,560
It's an annular rash that has erythematous borders,

81
00:03:07,560 --> 00:03:09,500
so hence the term marginatum,

82
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:11,600
because it's more red at the margins.

83
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:14,460
And finally, the S reminds us of syndenhems chorea.

84
00:03:14,460 --> 00:03:17,460
So recall that these patients can have rapid involuntary movements

85
00:03:17,460 --> 00:03:21,000
of their muscle, which, of course, is called chorea.

86
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:24,240
And so these are the five major criteria of acute rheumatic fever.

87
00:03:24,240 --> 00:03:28,100
Now, of these five, the joint problems, the nodules, the erythema,

88
00:03:28,100 --> 00:03:31,340
and the syndenhems chorea will all resolve with time,

89
00:03:31,340 --> 00:03:33,520
and there are no long-term sequelae.

90
00:03:33,520 --> 00:03:36,620
But the key problem is going to be the heart problem,

91
00:03:36,620 --> 00:03:38,760
and so we want to focus on that a little bit.

92
00:03:38,760 --> 00:03:42,600
So let me just remind you that the heart problems are going to be a pancarditis.

93
00:03:42,600 --> 00:03:45,200
And by pancarditis, we mean that each of the layers of the heart

94
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:46,500
is going to be inflamed.

95
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:48,900
And so the first of the layers, or the innermost layer,

96
00:03:48,900 --> 00:03:52,180
would be the endocardium, and so they can get an endocarditis.

97
00:03:52,180 --> 00:03:53,540
Now, when they get an endocarditis,

98
00:03:53,540 --> 00:03:57,840
they're going to get these tiny little vegetations on the endocardium,

99
00:03:57,840 --> 00:04:01,720
particularly the endocardium of the mitral valve.

100
00:04:01,720 --> 00:04:05,060
And so the endocarditis will actually involve valves preferentially,

101
00:04:05,060 --> 00:04:08,000
and it's mostly going to involve the mitral valve.

102
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:10,240
Now, if there's an additional valve that will be involved,

103
00:04:10,240 --> 00:04:11,300
it'll be the aortic.

104
00:04:11,300 --> 00:04:14,100
So basically, it will involve the mitral valve,

105
00:04:14,100 --> 00:04:17,840
and if there's a second valve, it'll be the mitral plus the aortic.

106
00:04:17,840 --> 00:04:21,240
Now, when you get these little vegetations on the surface

107
00:04:21,240 --> 00:04:24,840
or the very edge of the valve, that's going to lead to regurgitation,

108
00:04:24,840 --> 00:04:28,480
and so one of the complications would be mitral valve regurgitation.

109
00:04:28,480 --> 00:04:30,360
So that's the endocarditis.

110
00:04:30,360 --> 00:04:32,320
Now, as far as the myocarditis is concerned,

111
00:04:32,320 --> 00:04:34,920
the key problem will be that you'll get these bodies

112
00:04:34,920 --> 00:04:37,580
within the myocardium called ashoff bodies.

113
00:04:37,580 --> 00:04:40,400
And this is an image here of an ashoff body,

114
00:04:40,400 --> 00:04:43,100
and you can see that we've got all these myocytes here.

115
00:04:43,100 --> 00:04:45,200
I'm labeling them with M.

116
00:04:45,200 --> 00:04:47,000
And then in the middle of these myocytes,

117
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:48,700
we've got this ashoff body.

118
00:04:48,700 --> 00:04:49,860
Now, what's an ashoff body?

119
00:04:49,860 --> 00:04:53,800
It is a focus of chronic inflammation with giant cells

120
00:04:53,800 --> 00:04:55,440
and fibrinoid material,

121
00:04:55,440 --> 00:04:58,580
which is really just all this degenerated collagen,

122
00:04:58,580 --> 00:05:02,520
with a group of cells called the anichcal cells.

123
00:05:02,520 --> 00:05:04,960
Now, the anichcal cells are particularly high yield,

124
00:05:04,960 --> 00:05:08,760
and what they are are these histiocytes, these cells here,

125
00:05:08,760 --> 00:05:12,100
with these slender wavy nuclei running down the middle.

126
00:05:12,100 --> 00:05:16,040
And so they also call that the caterpillar nucleus.

127
00:05:16,040 --> 00:05:19,600
Now, this is a hallmark cell, and examiners like to go after it.

128
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:21,540
And I'd like to just take you back and remind you that,

129
00:05:21,540 --> 00:05:23,040
now, you've got this ashoff body,

130
00:05:23,040 --> 00:05:24,740
and you're going to see some anichcal cells.

131
00:05:24,740 --> 00:05:26,520
For example, I'm circling one.

132
00:05:26,520 --> 00:05:29,760
And this is a very important finding in a patient

133
00:05:29,760 --> 00:05:31,400
with acute rheumatic fever.

134
00:05:31,400 --> 00:05:34,200
And that's because the myocarditis that occurs

135
00:05:34,200 --> 00:05:37,500
in acute rheumatic fever is the most common cause of death

136
00:05:37,500 --> 00:05:38,960
during the acute phase.

137
00:05:38,960 --> 00:05:41,200
So if there's one thing that's going to kill the patient

138
00:05:41,200 --> 00:05:42,460
with acute rheumatic fever,

139
00:05:42,460 --> 00:05:44,080
it's going to be the myocarditis.

140
00:05:44,080 --> 00:05:46,000
And what is the hallmark of the myocarditis

141
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:48,040
are going to be the ashoff bodies,

142
00:05:48,040 --> 00:05:50,320
which contain anichcal cells.

143
00:05:50,320 --> 00:05:52,680
So all of that is particularly high yield.

144
00:05:52,680 --> 00:05:54,880
Now, recall that I told you that there would also be

145
00:05:54,880 --> 00:05:58,720
pericarditis, and so the pericarditis can lead to a friction rub.

146
00:05:58,720 --> 00:06:01,460
And so these patients can also develop a friction rub.

147
00:06:01,460 --> 00:06:03,620
Now, the acute attack will eventually resolve,

148
00:06:03,620 --> 00:06:07,120
assuming that the patient doesn't die from the myocarditis,

149
00:06:07,120 --> 00:06:08,800
but it can progress to chronic disease.

150
00:06:08,800 --> 00:06:11,860
So you can actually develop chronic rheumatic heart disease.

151
00:06:11,860 --> 00:06:13,760
And that occurs when there's repeat exposure

152
00:06:13,760 --> 00:06:15,660
to group A beta hemolytic strep,

153
00:06:15,660 --> 00:06:18,200
which then results in a relapse of the acute phase.

154
00:06:18,200 --> 00:06:20,240
And each time you relapse the acute phase,

155
00:06:20,240 --> 00:06:23,640
it increases the risk for chronic rheumatic valvular disease.

156
00:06:23,640 --> 00:06:27,380
And chronic rheumatic valvular disease is basically scarring

157
00:06:27,380 --> 00:06:30,040
that occurs from a bunch of acute attacks.

158
00:06:30,040 --> 00:06:32,180
This scarring will result in stenosis,

159
00:06:32,180 --> 00:06:33,780
which means that you're going to tighten the valve

160
00:06:33,780 --> 00:06:36,320
and make it difficult to push anything through it.

161
00:06:36,320 --> 00:06:39,380
And it almost always involves a mitral valve.

162
00:06:39,380 --> 00:06:40,720
Now, let's go back and remind ourselves

163
00:06:40,720 --> 00:06:44,160
that acute rheumatic fever basically involves a mitral valve.

164
00:06:44,160 --> 00:06:45,900
And then if it's going to involve another valve,

165
00:06:45,900 --> 00:06:47,960
it'll involve the aortic beyond that.

166
00:06:47,960 --> 00:06:50,100
And so, of course, the chronic disease will also

167
00:06:50,100 --> 00:06:52,340
almost always involve the mitral valve.

168
00:06:52,340 --> 00:06:56,080
It'll lead to thickening of the corda tendinae in the cusps,

169
00:06:56,080 --> 00:06:59,080
and eventually that, of course, is going to produce stenosis.

170
00:06:59,080 --> 00:07:01,880
Now, occasionally, it can also involve the aortic valve,

171
00:07:01,880 --> 00:07:04,320
which would then lead to fusion of the commissures.

172
00:07:04,320 --> 00:07:06,380
I'll show you a picture of that in a moment.

173
00:07:06,380 --> 00:07:08,520
The other valves are less commonly involved.

174
00:07:08,520 --> 00:07:10,920
Now, when you've got damage to these valves,

175
00:07:10,920 --> 00:07:14,300
then the complication of a damaged valve will be endocarditis.

176
00:07:14,300 --> 00:07:15,800
And you're going to hear more about that

177
00:07:15,800 --> 00:07:18,600
in our discussions of endocarditis that are upcoming.

178
00:07:18,600 --> 00:07:20,340
Here's an image of the aortic valve

179
00:07:20,340 --> 00:07:22,840
that's involved by chronic rheumatic heart disease.

180
00:07:22,840 --> 00:07:25,680
Again, the idea here is that you get scarring of the valve

181
00:07:25,680 --> 00:07:26,940
with fusion of the commissures,

182
00:07:26,940 --> 00:07:28,580
and that's particularly high yield.

183
00:07:28,580 --> 00:07:30,080
So, let's go back and look at this.

184
00:07:30,080 --> 00:07:31,520
This is the normal aortic valve,

185
00:07:31,520 --> 00:07:33,640
and it should open like this,

186
00:07:33,640 --> 00:07:37,160
so that you should have cusps one, two, and three.

187
00:07:37,160 --> 00:07:39,620
And these cusps should sort of plug in

188
00:07:39,620 --> 00:07:43,220
to the very edge of the aortic valve root.

189
00:07:43,220 --> 00:07:45,200
Now, notice here that what's happening

190
00:07:45,200 --> 00:07:48,200
is you've got this scarring here and here,

191
00:07:48,200 --> 00:07:50,240
which is fusing these commissures,

192
00:07:50,240 --> 00:07:52,400
these single edges of the valve,

193
00:07:52,400 --> 00:07:54,400
which is fusing these commissures together.

194
00:07:54,400 --> 00:07:57,040
And so, when you get fusion of the commissures,

195
00:07:57,040 --> 00:08:00,620
what ends up happening is it reduces the orifice of the valve.

196
00:08:00,620 --> 00:08:03,440
It actually then results in the formation of this space

197
00:08:03,440 --> 00:08:05,120
that resembles a fish mouth,

198
00:08:05,120 --> 00:08:09,460
and so this is also known as the fish mouth appearance of the valve.

199
00:08:09,460 --> 00:08:10,720
Again, this is the aortic valve,

200
00:08:10,720 --> 00:08:12,360
and the problem is that you're going to have stenosis

201
00:08:12,360 --> 00:08:15,560
because you're not going to be able to completely open this valve.

202
00:08:15,560 --> 00:08:17,900
Switching gears and moving to aortic stenosis,

203
00:08:17,900 --> 00:08:20,940
this is a narrowing of the aortic valve orifice.

204
00:08:20,940 --> 00:08:22,700
Now, again, this is the left ventricle here,

205
00:08:22,700 --> 00:08:25,300
and of course, the aortic valve would be opening here.

206
00:08:25,300 --> 00:08:29,800
If you narrowed the opening or the window of this valve,

207
00:08:29,800 --> 00:08:31,900
that would be called aortic stenosis.

208
00:08:31,900 --> 00:08:35,080
Now, normally, the valve opening is about four centimeters squared,

209
00:08:35,080 --> 00:08:39,220
but in aortic stenosis, it becomes less than one centimeter squared.

210
00:08:39,220 --> 00:08:41,860
Now, this is usually due to fibrosis and calcification,

211
00:08:41,860 --> 00:08:44,700
so the idea is that it's a wear and tear disorder.

212
00:08:44,700 --> 00:08:48,400
The more you bang on the valve, the more you use the valve over time,

213
00:08:48,400 --> 00:08:52,300
the more it begins to fibrose and undergo dystrophic calcification,

214
00:08:52,300 --> 00:08:55,840
and eventually, that then is going to result in stenosis of the valve.

215
00:08:55,840 --> 00:08:58,040
This is going to present in late adulthood.

216
00:08:58,040 --> 00:09:00,480
Again, the longer you live, the more you bang on the valve,

217
00:09:00,480 --> 00:09:03,220
the more you degenerate the valve,

218
00:09:03,220 --> 00:09:05,580
the more likely the disease is to occur.

219
00:09:05,580 --> 00:09:08,820
And there's also an increased risk with bicuspid aortic valve.

220
00:09:08,820 --> 00:09:11,920
Now, remember that the aortic valve is normally a tricuspid valve.

221
00:09:11,920 --> 00:09:15,060
It has three cusps, and each of them is doing their job.

222
00:09:15,060 --> 00:09:19,900
If you have a bicuspid valve, then the two cusps have to do the work of three,

223
00:09:19,900 --> 00:09:22,300
and when the two cusps have to do the work of three,

224
00:09:22,300 --> 00:09:24,840
there's going to be an increased risk for aortic stenosis,

225
00:09:24,840 --> 00:09:28,480
and when it occurs, it'll occur earlier than the patients

226
00:09:28,480 --> 00:09:32,040
who have the normal three cusps of the aortic valve.

227
00:09:32,040 --> 00:09:33,940
Here's a picture of aortic stenosis.

228
00:09:33,940 --> 00:09:37,480
Notice that you've got three cusps to the valve,

229
00:09:37,480 --> 00:09:41,480
but also notice that this valve appears fibrotic and irregular.

230
00:09:41,480 --> 00:09:43,820
Normally, it should be absolutely smooth,

231
00:09:43,820 --> 00:09:47,320
i.e. each of the cusps should be absolutely smooth.

232
00:09:47,320 --> 00:09:49,360
And so this is an example of aortic stenosis,

233
00:09:49,360 --> 00:09:53,660
and you can see that it's decreasing the space through which blood can flow,

234
00:09:53,660 --> 00:09:56,300
i.e. the window has become tight.

235
00:09:56,300 --> 00:09:59,940
Now, we can also get aortic stenosis from chronic rheumatic valve disease.

236
00:09:59,940 --> 00:10:02,380
So, as I stated just a few slides back,

237
00:10:02,380 --> 00:10:05,440
one of the complications of chronic rheumatic valve disease is stenosis,

238
00:10:05,440 --> 00:10:09,440
and you get stenosis of both the mitral valve as well as the aortic valve.

239
00:10:09,440 --> 00:10:13,280
Now, how are we going to distinguish stenosis from rheumatic disease

240
00:10:13,280 --> 00:10:16,280
versus stenosis from wear and tear?

241
00:10:16,280 --> 00:10:18,280
Well, first of all, whenever you have rheumatic disease,

242
00:10:18,280 --> 00:10:20,660
you're always going to have mitral stenosis as well,

243
00:10:20,660 --> 00:10:24,700
because remember that rheumatic valvular disease always starts in the mitral valve,

244
00:10:24,700 --> 00:10:27,400
and then may or may not involve the aortic valve.

245
00:10:27,400 --> 00:10:30,300
And so if you've got stenosis of the aortic valve by itself,

246
00:10:30,300 --> 00:10:32,160
it's not going to be rheumatic heart disease.

247
00:10:32,160 --> 00:10:35,600
You've got to have stenosis of the mitral valve plus the aortic valve,

248
00:10:35,600 --> 00:10:36,940
and that's the first point.

249
00:10:36,940 --> 00:10:40,440
The second point is that the aortic valve will have fusion of the commissures

250
00:10:40,440 --> 00:10:43,540
when there is chronic rheumatic heart disease.

251
00:10:43,540 --> 00:10:49,680
Here again is a picture of the aortic valve from the patient with chronic rheumatic heart disease,

252
00:10:49,680 --> 00:10:52,380
and remember that we had fusion of these commissures,

253
00:10:52,380 --> 00:10:55,180
where the edges of the commissures got stuck together,

254
00:10:55,180 --> 00:10:57,420
creating this fish mouth appearance.

255
00:10:57,420 --> 00:11:01,520
For the purposes of comparing and contrasting, here is the aortic valve

256
00:11:01,520 --> 00:11:03,920
in which you've had extensive wear and tear.

257
00:11:03,920 --> 00:11:07,300
Now notice that there is no fusion of the commissures,

258
00:11:07,300 --> 00:11:11,600
and again that's a major distinction from chronic rheumatic valve disease.

259
00:11:11,600 --> 00:11:14,240
Regardless of the cause of aortic stenosis,

260
00:11:14,240 --> 00:11:17,840
the patients will have a prolonged asymptomatic stage.

261
00:11:17,840 --> 00:11:19,840
That's because there's going to be compensation,

262
00:11:19,840 --> 00:11:25,380
such that the left ventricle will be able to push blood across that stenotic area.

263
00:11:25,380 --> 00:11:28,980
When the left ventricle is pushing against the stenotic area,

264
00:11:28,980 --> 00:11:31,020
it will eventually blow open that valve,

265
00:11:31,020 --> 00:11:34,380
and when it blows it open, you'll hear a systolic ejection click,

266
00:11:34,380 --> 00:11:36,020
and then as the valve opens,

267
00:11:36,020 --> 00:11:40,060
all the blood will sort of move out in a big rush, giving you a crescendo,

268
00:11:40,060 --> 00:11:43,960
and then eventually that will decrease, giving you a decrescendo murmur.

269
00:11:43,960 --> 00:11:46,740
And so these are some of the hallmarks of aortic stenosis.

270
00:11:46,740 --> 00:11:51,180
The complications of aortic stenosis include concentric left ventricular hypertrophy,

271
00:11:51,180 --> 00:11:54,040
and so again, if this is the left ventricle here,

272
00:11:54,040 --> 00:11:58,280
and the left ventricle has to pump against this tight stenotic aortic valve,

273
00:11:58,280 --> 00:12:03,580
you're going to get hypertrophy of the muscle of the left ventricle.

274
00:12:03,580 --> 00:12:07,280
In addition, patients can get angina and syncope with exercise.

275
00:12:07,280 --> 00:12:10,280
The idea here is that because there is a stenosis,

276
00:12:10,280 --> 00:12:14,300
there is a limited ability to increase blood flow across the valve,

277
00:12:14,300 --> 00:12:16,100
and so therefore if there's exercise,

278
00:12:16,100 --> 00:12:19,840
it's difficult to get extra blood to the brain and the heart,

279
00:12:19,840 --> 00:12:22,840
and that can result in angina and syncope.

280
00:12:22,840 --> 00:12:26,240
And finally, there can be a microangiopathic hemolytic anemia.

281
00:12:26,240 --> 00:12:30,980
The idea here is that when the blood flows across the degenerated calcified valve,

282
00:12:30,980 --> 00:12:32,780
some of the blood cells can rupture,

283
00:12:32,780 --> 00:12:36,620
and that would give you a microangiopathic hemolytic anemia.

284
00:12:36,620 --> 00:12:38,900
And so these are all important complications.

285
00:12:38,900 --> 00:12:41,660
The treatment is based on the presence of the complications,

286
00:12:41,660 --> 00:12:47,300
so you normally don't replace the valve until after the onset of the complications.

287
00:12:47,300 --> 00:12:51,640
Moving on to aortic regurgitation, let's illustrate this.

288
00:12:51,640 --> 00:12:54,840
Here is the left atrium and the left ventricle,

289
00:12:54,840 --> 00:13:00,480
and recall that the left ventricle will push blood across the aortic valve during systole.

290
00:13:00,480 --> 00:13:04,960
Now during diastole, this should seal, allowing for the blood to move forward.

291
00:13:04,960 --> 00:13:09,380
However, if there is leakiness of this valve, that will result in regurgitation,

292
00:13:09,380 --> 00:13:11,700
allowing some blood to move backward.

293
00:13:11,700 --> 00:13:14,700
And that's the definition of aortic regurgitation.

294
00:13:14,700 --> 00:13:20,040
This is the fact that the back flow of blood from the aorta into the left ventricle during diastole.

295
00:13:20,040 --> 00:13:25,980
Now the most common cause of aortic regurgitation is a isolated aortic root dilatation.

296
00:13:25,980 --> 00:13:30,720
So the idea here is that if this is the aortic root, and coming off the root are the valves,

297
00:13:30,720 --> 00:13:37,280
then if there is a dilation of this aortic root, that is going to pull on the valves,

298
00:13:37,280 --> 00:13:44,620
which will then increase the space between the valves, allowing for regurgitation backward into the left ventricle.

299
00:13:44,620 --> 00:13:49,560
And so this is the most common cause, an isolated aortic root dilatation.

300
00:13:49,560 --> 00:13:54,440
At the same time, you can get aortic root dilatation with a syphilitic aneurysm.

301
00:13:54,440 --> 00:13:56,560
Remember the picture that I drew you during the vascular lectures.

302
00:13:56,560 --> 00:14:04,900
If this is the aorta coming off the left ventricle, then if there is an aneurysm of the aorta in the thorax,

303
00:14:04,900 --> 00:14:09,840
this aneurysm, or balloon-like dilatation, will pull on the aortic root.

304
00:14:09,840 --> 00:14:13,140
And when it pulls on the aortic root, that's going to make this valve leaky,

305
00:14:13,140 --> 00:14:16,720
so that blood will actually go backward during diastole.

306
00:14:16,720 --> 00:14:18,720
And so this is another important cause.

307
00:14:18,720 --> 00:14:23,560
But of course, it's not the most common cause, but it's another way by which you can dilate the aortic root.

308
00:14:23,560 --> 00:14:29,200
And finally, if you damage the valve itself, if this is the aortic root, you've got these cusps of the valves coming off.

309
00:14:29,200 --> 00:14:33,540
If you damage these valves, that will then result in aortic regurgitation.

310
00:14:33,540 --> 00:14:40,480
And the example would be infectious endocarditis, which could potentially damage the valve, resulting in aortic regurgitation.

311
00:14:40,480 --> 00:14:45,120
Now the clinical features will be that you'll have an early blowing diastolic murmur.

312
00:14:45,120 --> 00:14:49,280
Now diastolic murmur, because the blood will go back during diastole.

313
00:14:49,280 --> 00:14:53,920
And whenever you have blood going backwards, you often get a blowing murmur.

314
00:14:53,920 --> 00:14:58,920
An additional clinical feature is that the patients will have something called a hyperdynamic circulation.

315
00:14:58,920 --> 00:15:01,700
And this is particularly high yield to be aware of.

316
00:15:01,700 --> 00:15:07,460
The idea here is that you've got the heart, and of course left atrium, left ventricle.

317
00:15:07,460 --> 00:15:14,100
Let's just make up an arbitrary number and pretend that the heart pumps 100 milliliters each time it squeezes.

318
00:15:14,100 --> 00:15:23,740
Now that 100 milliliters is going to go into the aorta, and then of course that's going to generate a blood pressure as it goes down through the rest of the system.

319
00:15:23,740 --> 00:15:34,620
In aortic regurgitation, you're going to get some regurgitation of volume, so that this 100 milliliters now becomes 80, and so that would decrease the diastolic pressure.

320
00:15:34,620 --> 00:15:46,260
And at the same time, with the return of 20 milliliters to the left ventricle, the next time it's going to pump out 120, and that's going to increase the stroke volume, and of course the stroke volume is going to then increase the systolic pressure.

321
00:15:46,260 --> 00:15:58,140
So you're going to get an increase in the systolic pressure and a decrease in the diastolic pressure, and that is then going to widen something called the pulse pressure, which is the difference between the systolic and diastolic pressures.

322
00:15:58,140 --> 00:16:10,420
Now, as there's this widening of the pulse pressure, the patients are going to have bounding pulses, they're going to have a pulsatile nail bed, they often can have head bobbing, especially on board examinations, and all of this is called a hyperdynamic circulation,

323
00:16:10,420 --> 00:16:23,660
and it's based on the fact that the systolic pressure increases because of the increased stroke volume with the regurgitation, and the diastolic pressure decreases due to the fact that some blood is being regurgitated back into the heart.

324
00:16:23,660 --> 00:16:36,000
Additional clinical features is that you'll get left ventricular dilatation, as you add additional volume back into the left ventricle, the left ventricle can dilate, and one of the important features of volume overload is that you get hypertrophy,

325
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:48,580
but it's an eccentric hypertrophy, where it involves one aspect of the ventricle as opposed to the entire concentric ventricle, and so they call that eccentric hypertrophy, and it's classically seen with volume overloaded states.

326
00:16:48,580 --> 00:16:53,580
Now, the treatment will be valve replacement once left ventricular dysfunction develops.

327
00:16:53,580 --> 00:16:58,580
Mitral valve prolapse is ballooning of the mitral valve into the left atrium during systole.

328
00:16:58,580 --> 00:17:06,580
Let's just briefly illustrate this, so here you've got the left atrium, and this is the left ventricle, and then you've got the mitral valve here.

329
00:17:06,580 --> 00:17:13,580
Now, this mitral valve is normally very tight, and so you can think of it sort of as a very firm kind of rubbery substance.

330
00:17:13,580 --> 00:17:23,580
However, if there's degeneration of the valve, then that firm rubber would actually turn into this more myxoid or gel-like substance, making the valve more loose and floppy.

331
00:17:23,580 --> 00:17:30,580
And that's what happens in mitral valve prolapse, is that you get myxoid degeneration of the valve, making it more floppy.

332
00:17:30,580 --> 00:17:32,580
The etiology is usually not known.

333
00:17:32,580 --> 00:17:37,580
However, it can be associated with, for example, Marfan syndrome or Ehlers-Danlos syndrome.

334
00:17:37,580 --> 00:17:40,580
But in most cases, the etiology is not known.

335
00:17:40,580 --> 00:17:50,580
Now, the idea here is that when this valve becomes floppy, then what's going to happen is that when the left ventricle is squeezing blood out down through the aortic valve,

336
00:17:50,580 --> 00:17:56,580
this valve should normally become very tight, i.e., the mitral valve should normally become very tight and should close.

337
00:17:56,580 --> 00:18:02,580
However, in this particular case, there's going to be a little bit of a ballooning backward of the mitral valve during systole,

338
00:18:02,580 --> 00:18:07,580
and when that mitral valve balloons backwards, you're going to get a little bit of a click.

339
00:18:07,580 --> 00:18:09,580
It's sort of like opening of a parachute.

340
00:18:09,580 --> 00:18:16,580
And so when you open a parachute, you'd hear a loud noise, and in the same way, during systole, as this valve parachutes backwards,

341
00:18:16,580 --> 00:18:20,580
you're going to hear a click in the middle of systole.

342
00:18:20,580 --> 00:18:23,580
And that's one of the key clinical features, a mid-systolic click.

343
00:18:23,580 --> 00:18:27,580
Now, in addition, there can be some regurgitation back across that valve.

344
00:18:27,580 --> 00:18:30,580
There doesn't always have to be, but it often is present.

345
00:18:30,580 --> 00:18:38,580
The complications of mitral valve prolapse are rare, but they include infectious endocarditis because the valve will be damaged over time,

346
00:18:38,580 --> 00:18:41,580
arrhythmia, and severe mitral regurgitation.

347
00:18:41,580 --> 00:18:47,580
Of course, as this valve is clicking backwards, it's going to get stretched, and as it gets stretched,

348
00:18:47,580 --> 00:18:54,580
that's going to result in some leakiness to this valve, which would then result in mitral valve regurs.

349
00:18:54,580 --> 00:18:56,580
The treatment is valve replacement.

350
00:18:56,580 --> 00:19:00,580
The final point I'd like to make concerning mitral valve prolapse is this is a picture of mitral valve prolapse,

351
00:19:00,580 --> 00:19:07,580
and you can see that this valve has become very loose and floppy, and it's sort of regurgitating back towards you,

352
00:19:07,580 --> 00:19:13,580
because we're actually sitting in the atrium here looking at the valve as it comes back towards us.

353
00:19:13,580 --> 00:19:19,580
Mitral regurgitation is when you get reflux of blood from the left ventricle into the left atrium during systole.

354
00:19:19,580 --> 00:19:24,580
Again, the idea here is that you've got the left atrium here, you've got the left ventricle here.

355
00:19:24,580 --> 00:19:31,580
This valve should normally become tight and shut when the left ventricle is squeezing and pushing blood forward during systole.

356
00:19:31,580 --> 00:19:37,580
However, if this valve becomes loose, then during systole some of the blood can actually go backwards into the left atrium,

357
00:19:37,580 --> 00:19:39,580
and that's called mitral valve regurg.

358
00:19:39,580 --> 00:19:45,580
This usually arises as a complication of mitral valve prolapse, as I just stated in the prior slides.

359
00:19:45,580 --> 00:19:48,580
Other causes would include left ventricular dilatation.

360
00:19:48,580 --> 00:19:53,580
Again, if this is the standard picture that we keep drawing, and this is the left atrium and the left ventricle,

361
00:19:53,580 --> 00:20:01,580
if you get dilation of the left ventricle, you're actually going to pull apart the leaflets of this valve, making this valve leaky.

362
00:20:01,580 --> 00:20:06,580
Other causes listed here include infective endocarditis, which can damage the valve leaflets,

363
00:20:06,580 --> 00:20:08,580
acute rheumatic heart disease.

364
00:20:08,580 --> 00:20:13,580
Remember that in acute rheumatic heart disease, the most commonly involved valve is the mitral valve,

365
00:20:13,580 --> 00:20:19,580
and the most common complication is where you get those little vegetations on the valve, resulting in mitral valve regurgitation.

366
00:20:19,580 --> 00:20:25,580
And finally, papillary muscle rupture after a myocardial infarction can also give you mitral valve regurgitation.

367
00:20:25,580 --> 00:20:29,580
Here again, you've got the left atrium, and this is the left ventricle.

368
00:20:29,580 --> 00:20:36,580
Remember that the mitral valve has these little string-like tendine coming off the valve,

369
00:20:36,580 --> 00:20:40,580
which are called the corda tendine, and they attach to the papillary muscles here.

370
00:20:40,580 --> 00:20:45,580
And the papillary muscles help to keep this valve shut and closed during systole.

371
00:20:45,580 --> 00:20:50,580
Now, if there's rupture of a papillary muscle, then you would not be able to keep this valve closed,

372
00:20:50,580 --> 00:20:54,580
and you could get regurgitation back across that valve.

373
00:20:54,580 --> 00:20:57,580
Clinically, the patients will have a holosystolic blowing murmur.

374
00:20:57,580 --> 00:21:02,580
The idea here is that when the left ventricle is trying to squeeze blood forward during systole,

375
00:21:02,580 --> 00:21:04,580
some of the blood would go backward.

376
00:21:04,580 --> 00:21:08,580
Now, this could get louder with squatting or with expiration.

377
00:21:08,580 --> 00:21:13,580
And the idea here is that if this is the left atrium and this is the left ventricle,

378
00:21:13,580 --> 00:21:18,580
and the patient squats, when they squat, they're going to increase the resistance here.

379
00:21:18,580 --> 00:21:21,580
So if less blood is going to be able to go forward because there's a higher pressure,

380
00:21:21,580 --> 00:21:24,580
more blood will be squeezed this way.

381
00:21:24,580 --> 00:21:27,580
And so the murmur would become louder when they're squatting.

382
00:21:27,580 --> 00:21:30,580
In addition, the murmur will get louder with expiration.

383
00:21:30,580 --> 00:21:35,580
The idea here is that when there's expiration, there's going to be an increase in the amount of blood

384
00:21:35,580 --> 00:21:37,580
that enters into the left atrium.

385
00:21:37,580 --> 00:21:41,580
That will then result in an increase in the blood in the left ventricle.

386
00:21:41,580 --> 00:21:45,580
Now, during that cycle, when this increased blood is pushed this way,

387
00:21:45,580 --> 00:21:48,580
there will also be increased blood regurgitating back this way,

388
00:21:48,580 --> 00:21:51,580
which is going to make the murmur louder.

389
00:21:51,580 --> 00:21:54,580
Mitral stenosis is when we have narrowing of the mitral valve orifice,

390
00:21:54,580 --> 00:21:59,580
and the key circumstance in which we would see this would be chronic rheumatic valve disease.

391
00:21:59,580 --> 00:22:02,580
Remember that chronic rheumatic valve disease preferentially involves the mitral valve,

392
00:22:02,580 --> 00:22:04,580
and that it creates stenosis.

393
00:22:04,580 --> 00:22:10,580
So chronic rheumatic disease creates stenosis, and acute rheumatic disease creates regurg.

394
00:22:10,580 --> 00:22:13,580
And that's a high-yield sort of simple principle to bear in mind.

395
00:22:13,580 --> 00:22:18,580
Now, what you're going to get here is an opening snap followed by a diastolic rumble.

396
00:22:18,580 --> 00:22:20,580
Now, the idea here is very simple.

397
00:22:20,580 --> 00:22:24,580
If this is the left atrium, and this is the left ventricle, and there's a stenosis here,

398
00:22:24,580 --> 00:22:29,580
then the left atrium will push open the stenosis during diastole,

399
00:22:29,580 --> 00:22:31,580
when it's feeding blood to the left ventricle,

400
00:22:31,580 --> 00:22:35,580
and you'll get a little bit of a rumble after you blow open that window.

401
00:22:35,580 --> 00:22:38,580
And so that's the key finding, as we've stated in the text,

402
00:22:38,580 --> 00:22:41,580
an opening snap followed by a diastolic rumble.

403
00:22:41,580 --> 00:22:47,580
One of the key complications of mitral stenosis is that you'll get volume overload of the left atrium.

404
00:22:47,580 --> 00:22:52,580
Again, all of the blood in the left atrium will not be able to be pushed into the left ventricle

405
00:22:52,580 --> 00:22:53,580
because of the stenosis.

406
00:22:53,580 --> 00:22:58,580
And so eventually, you'll get volume overload with dilatation of the left atrium.

407
00:22:58,580 --> 00:23:01,580
Now, the complications of this will be the following.

408
00:23:01,580 --> 00:23:03,580
Number one, we'll get pulmonary congestion.

409
00:23:03,580 --> 00:23:05,580
And that's because as you overload the left atrium,

410
00:23:05,580 --> 00:23:08,580
you're also going to back up blood into the pulmonary circuit,

411
00:23:08,580 --> 00:23:10,580
and so that'll result in pulmonary congestion.

412
00:23:10,580 --> 00:23:15,580
And remember, the two complications of pulmonary congestion are going to be edema,

413
00:23:15,580 --> 00:23:20,580
because we're going to increase the hydrostatic pressure in the vessels of the lung.

414
00:23:20,580 --> 00:23:22,580
And number two, alveolar hemorrhage.

415
00:23:22,580 --> 00:23:24,580
As the capillaries become engorged, they can rupture.

416
00:23:24,580 --> 00:23:26,580
That will result in hemorrhage.

417
00:23:26,580 --> 00:23:29,580
The macrophages within the airspace will consume the blood,

418
00:23:29,580 --> 00:23:34,580
giving you hemocytorin-laden macrophages, also called heart failure cells.

419
00:23:34,580 --> 00:23:37,580
And so those are the two complications of pulmonary congestion.

420
00:23:37,580 --> 00:23:41,580
In addition, because there's excess blood volume within the pulmonary circuit,

421
00:23:41,580 --> 00:23:43,580
we'll get pulmonary hypertension.

422
00:23:43,580 --> 00:23:45,580
Of course, if there's pulmonary hypertension,

423
00:23:45,580 --> 00:23:48,580
the right heart will have to pump against the pulmonary hypertension,

424
00:23:48,580 --> 00:23:51,580
and eventually the right heart could fail.

425
00:23:51,580 --> 00:23:56,580
And then finally, because there is going to be dilatation of the left atrium,

426
00:23:56,580 --> 00:24:00,580
as you stretch that atrium, you're going to get abnormalities of the conducting system,

427
00:24:00,580 --> 00:24:03,580
which is then going to result in atrial fibrillation.

428
00:24:03,580 --> 00:24:06,580
And when you get atrial fibrillation, you're going to have abnormal movement of the wall,

429
00:24:06,580 --> 00:24:11,580
which will result in stasis, which will then increase the risk of mural thrombi.

430
00:24:11,580 --> 00:24:15,580
And so here is the classic image of a mural thrombus in the left atrium.

431
00:24:15,580 --> 00:24:17,580
You can see this thrombus here up against the wall,

432
00:24:17,580 --> 00:24:21,580
and it's due to the fact that you've got poor movement of the atrial wall,

433
00:24:21,580 --> 00:24:25,580
resulting in stasis of the blood, increasing the risk of thrombosis.

434
00:24:25,580 --> 00:24:27,580
And so this is called a mural thrombus.

435
00:24:27,580 --> 00:24:30,580
And that concludes our discussion on valvular disorders.

